{"A":"Research a zero-target fast path atop the 56dc… design: combine lookup/increment for identical prefix sums, minimize probes, and retain the general path. Also test branch/layout refinements to the fixed table; preserve full int-range keys and all dimensions.","B":"Research lower-overhead, load-balanced 4-core work partitioning for the 56dc… rolling-strip kernel. Compare dynamic first-boundary scheduling with weighted static partitions of the triangular work; retain SIMD accumulation and thread-private state.","C":"Research the frequency-counter hot loop independently: probe-count reduction, hash mixing/layout choices, and safe small-scan specializations. Compare against 56dc… rather than assuming prefix-precompute or flattened-pair variants help.","G":"Use 56dc74b8b8b4c2b9c6c9c238c4f9a4e7218f0455 as the measured starting point. Integrate only individually validated improvements, never mechanically merge trees; remeasure complete combinations and verify all valid dimensions, negative values, extreme sums, and large answer counts.","analysis":"The best measured program is B (0.02131 s); C is close (0.02164 s), while the A approaches are much slower. The shared winning structure is min-dimension pairing, contiguous rolling strips, OpenMP, and timestamped fixed hashing. Further work should target its hash and scheduling hot paths, with a guarded k==0 specialization; full-program measurements decide integration."}